First Author: Dalton
Title: Refining the Lung Allocation Score Models Fails to Improve Discrimination Performance
Source number: 10  ￼

Very short summary: This retrospective SRTR study tested whether alternative statistical and machine-learning approaches could improve lung allocation survival modeling over current LAS/CAS models. Five key findings were: (1) 13,204 waitlist candidates and 20,763 post-transplant recipients were analyzed; (2) seven waitlist and seven post-transplant models were compared, including Cox, spline-enhanced Cox, random survival forests, logistic regression, linear discriminant analysis, and gradient-boosted trees; (3) waitlist discrimination was high at baseline and similar across models, with AUC about 0.93 at 1 month, declining over longer horizons and in residual cohorts; (4) post-transplant discrimination was poor across all models, generally around AUC 0.58-0.61, and did not improve with alternative machine-learning methods; and (5) subgroup analyses showed the greatest AUC variability for patients with Medicaid insurance. Tables 1-4 and Figure 1 were used directly for this extraction.

